  <h2>How to use this sheet</h2>
  <p>Work from top to bottom: first observe (A1), then weigh (A2), then diagnose (A3), and only then summarise (A4). The direction — receiving or forgetting — is the horizon, not the starting point.</p>
  <h2>Basic data</h2>
  <p>Parashah / scope: Ha'azinu — Deuteronomy 32:1–52. Haftarah (Shabbat Shuvah): Hosea 14:2–10 + Joel 2:15–27. Flanking reading and status: Matthew 18:21–35 — New Testament reflection, used confessionally (not as peshat). Audience: study group / congregation.</p>

  <h2>A1. Text blocks and peshat</h2>
  <table>
  <tr><th>Block</th><th>What it says (peshat)</th><th>Keywords / contrast</th><th>Open question</th></tr>
  <tr><td>32:1–6</td><td>Heaven and earth as witnesses; rain/dew; the Rock against a crooked generation.</td><td>witnesses · Rock</td><td>Do I begin with myself or with the Rock?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32:7–14</td><td>Remember; God found and carried like an eagle; abundance.</td><td>remember · eagle</td><td>Does God protect through movement?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32:15–18</td><td>Jeshurun waxed fat, forgot the Rock that begat him.</td><td>fat · forgetting</td><td>Why is abundance dangerous?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32:19–43</td><td>Judgment within faithfulness; “I kill and make alive, I heal.”</td><td>judgment · none other</td><td>Can judgment stand within faithfulness?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32:44–52</td><td>“No empty word, your life”; Moses to Nebo.</td><td>no empty word · Nebo</td><td>Do I myself stand under the Word?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hos. 14 / Joel 2</td><td>Return; take words; dew; rend your heart; gather all.</td><td>shuv · heart ↔ garments</td><td>What is form, what is heart?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Matt. 18:21–35</td><td>Forgiven servant seizes fellow servant by the throat.</td><td>forgiveness · hard hand</td><td>Where does forgetting become visible?</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>A2. Connecting hypotheses</h2>
  <p>Hypothesis 1 (one axis): the centre is Source alone — all is gift; the human receives. History and response stay in the background.</p>
  <p>Hypothesis 2 (relation): the centre is Source ↔ Trajectory, with a Coherence hinge — the unchanging Rock against a heart that forgets over time, and that forgetting becomes visible in how one treats the other (Matt. 18).</p>
  <p>Which explains the most data with the least strain? Hypothesis 2. It explains the historical character of the song (Trajectory), the reversal when Jeshurun “waxed fat” (32:15), and why Matt. 18 was chosen. Hypothesis 1 leaves the reversal and the gospel unexplained.</p>
  <p>What would refute this reading? If the song knew no reversal and Matt. 18 stood apart from the forgetting-motif, the weight would lie with Source alone.</p>

  <h2>A3. Four axes</h2>
  <table>
  <tr><th>Axis</th><th>Text anchors</th><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Open anomaly</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Source</td><td>32:2,4,18,39 · Hos. 14:5,8</td><td>Load-bearing. The Rock gives, carries, heals; “from Me your fruit.”</td><td>Why does He hide His face?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trajectory</td><td>32:7–15 · 32:26–27 · Hos. 14:1</td><td>Load-bearing. Found → fat → forgotten → judged → returned.</td><td>Abundance or wilderness the greater test?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Coherence</td><td>32:18 · Joel 2:13 · Matt. 18:32–33</td><td>Hinge. Forgetful heart ↔ hard hand.</td><td>Does Matt. 18 serve the centre?</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Will</td><td>32:1,46–47 · Hos. 14:2</td><td>Response, not source: listen, return, take words.</td><td>Where does returning become a project?</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>A4. Movement</h2>
  <p>What is unmasked? Forgetting the Rock in abundance — receiving that turns into possessing — and the treacherous idolatry that does not reject God but makes Him practically unnecessary; visible in the hard hand toward the neighbour (Matt. 18).</p>
  <p>What frame does the text put in its place? The unchanging Rock who gives and heals; teshuvah as looking again at Him, and “from Me is your fruit found.”</p>
  <p>What concrete response and formation follow? Listen (ha'azinu); return with words (Hosea); rend your heart, not your garments (Joel); and let the received forgiveness open the hand toward the other (Matt. 18).</p>
  <p><strong>Core sentence:</strong> The Rock does not change; the heart forgets — return, and let the grace you received open the hand you stretch out to your brother.</p>
